      <description>&lt;div id="storytext" class="storytext storylocation linkLocation"&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Good morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/newsletter/news" target="_blank"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; here to get it delivered to your inbox, and &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510318/up-first/" target="_blank"&gt;listen&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; to the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Today's top stories&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The confirmed death toll from flash floods that hit the Nepal-Tibet border has surpassed 460&lt;/strong&gt; people, and more than 1,500 people are reported missing. Around 90 Americans are &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/27/nx-s1-5946309/nepal-tibet-deadly-floods" target="_blank"&gt;among the people unaccounted for&lt;/a&gt;, according to the U.S. State Department. The U.S. Geological Survey says a &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/28/nx-s1-5945799/nepal-floods-climate-change-himalayas" target="_blank"&gt;glacial collapse&lt;/a&gt; triggered the floods. The State Department said it would provide the region with $500,000 in aid for the rescue efforts.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-140417" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 5616;         --source-height: 3159;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/5616x3159+0+293/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F2e%2Fe6%2F700f2fc245d293b243f1ae04b7f6%2Fap26239261112619.jpg" class="img" alt="Nepalese police officers stand in thick mud as earthmovers clear the debris of flash flooding." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Nepalese police officers watch as earthmovers clear the debris of flash flooding in Nepal's Nuwakot district on Thursday.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Niranjan Shrestha/AP                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧&lt;strong&gt; Search and rescue efforts are ongoing as receding waters leave behind mud and debris&lt;/strong&gt;, NPR's Diaa Hadid tells &lt;em&gt;Up First&lt;/em&gt;. Rasuwa, one of the hardest-hit regions, remains accessible only by helicopter. Authorities warn of &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-5947775:x" target="_blank"&gt;potential secondary floods&lt;/a&gt; due to an overflowing lake in the area where the first glacier collapsed. Experts are concerned that the lake may burst. Nepali authorities have rescued more than 1,500 people, but it remains unclear if they are accounted for on the lists of missing persons.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Federal Reserve Chairman Kevin Warsh is set to give his first major speech &lt;/strong&gt;as Fed Chair when he addresses economists and policymakers this morning at Jackson Lake Lodge in Wyoming. Investors were left feeling let down last month when he promised to curb inflation without outlining specific plans. Many are eager for clearer guidance today, as Fed chairs have historically used this gathering to share their economic views. Prices are climbing faster than the central bank's target. They increased &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/27/nx-s1-5945044/federal-reserve-kevin-warsh-jackson-hole-wyoming-bond-markets" target="_blank"&gt;3.7% in the 12 months&lt;/a&gt; ending in July.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Warsh's somewhat elusive communication style is quite different from his predecessors&lt;/strong&gt;, so it's unclear whether he will use today to &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-5947775:x" target="_blank"&gt;help fill in the blanks&lt;/a&gt; people want answers to, NPR's Scott Horsley says. After the last Fed meeting in July, Warsh said he was still deciding whether to focus this speech on the next few months of policy decisions or provide a more long-range perspective. Many consumers are concerned about current prices at the gas pump and in grocery stores and want answers on how the Fed plans to address these issues, Horsley says.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A federal judge heard arguments yesterday concerning the Kennedy Center &lt;/strong&gt;board's recent efforts to reinstate President Trump's name at the entrance of the arts complex. On Aug. 13, the board, dominated by members appointed by the president, voted to close the main building for renovations. It also voted to put up a new sign on the entryway that would read "The John F. Kennedy Memorial Center for the Performing Arts, Restored and Renovated by President Donald J. Trump." It said in a legal filing earlier this week that the &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/27/nx-s1-5945238/emergency-hearing-kennedy-center-demolish-threat" target="_blank"&gt;complex could be demolished&lt;/a&gt; if its plans for a two-year renovation, including the return of Trump's name, don't advance.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧&lt;strong&gt; The board has since emailed NPR's Anastasia Tsioulcas, walking back&lt;/strong&gt; on what they called a "hypothetical scenario." Rep. Joyce Beatty, a Democrat from Ohio, filed a federal lawsuit last December regarding the proposed renaming of the Kennedy Center. Judge Christopher R. Cooper has already ruled that renaming it the Trump Kennedy Center is unlawful, since only Congress has the authority to rename the center. The core question now is whether adding Trump's name in the way the board voted on this month is essentially the &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-5947775:x" target="_blank"&gt;same as renaming the center&lt;/a&gt;, Tsioulcas says. Beatty and her team argue that it is. The board and Trump say that a significant renovation is necessary, and adding his name does not equate to changing the center's identity.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Deep dive&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-140258" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4176;         --source-height: 2349;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4176x2349+0+218/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F11%2F4e%2F1228839e4b80800848e7b334b362%2Fap25294842226742.jpg" class="img" alt="An order of ground beef is prepared for a customer at Deep Cuts butcher shop in Dallas on Oct. 21, 2025." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 An order of ground beef is prepared for a customer at Deep Cuts butcher shop in Dallas on Oct. 21, 2025.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Tony Gutierrez/AP                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Trump is trying to beef up Americans' lives with a plan to temporarily lower tariff rates on some imported ground beef. In a social media post, the president said that the U.S. would "allow up to 300,000 metric tons of product for ground beef to be imported with no out of quota tariff." He also said the U.S. was committed to selling this beef at "25 percent below market prices." Beef has seen significant price increases due to rising inflation. Last month, U.S. consumers faced an average price of $6.89 per pound, and agricultural industry experts doubt that the president's plan will lead to a noticeable price drop. &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/26/nx-s1-5944265/ground-beef-trump-tariffs" target="_blank"&gt;Here's why&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🍔 The lower tariff rate applies to a relatively small portion of ground beef compared to the high demand for red meat in America.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🍔 The announcement didn't clarify whether the imported ground beef would add to the existing supply for the American market or would instead be a product arriving in the U.S. anyway that is now benefiting from a lower tariff rate.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🍔 Shoppers could see lower prices for a short time, but shouldn't expect major savings. According to Andrew Griffith, a University of Tennessee professor who studies livestock economics, a 25-cent price decrease would mean an annual savings of $15 for a typical U.S. consumer.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Weekend picks&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resnx-s1-img-6213907" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 1920;         --source-height: 1080;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1920x1080+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F40%2Fb3%2F50a71b3644939036d55ab564b6a1%2F080bd92d-5322-42b9-8b6b-1ac271db12e7.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1920x1080+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F40%2Fb3%2F50a71b3644939036d55ab564b6a1%2F080bd92d-5322-42b9-8b6b-1ac271db12e7.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1920x1080+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F40%2Fb3%2F50a71b3644939036d55ab564b6a1%2F080bd92d-5322-42b9-8b6b-1ac271db12e7.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/1920x1080+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F40%2Fb3%2F50a71b3644939036d55ab564b6a1%2F080bd92d-5322-42b9-8b6b-1ac271db12e7.jpg 900w, http