      <description>&lt;div id="storytext" class="storytext storylocation linkLocation"&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Good morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/newsletter/news" target="_blank"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; here to get it delivered to your inbox, and &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510318/up-first/" target="_blank"&gt;listen&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; to the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Today's top stories&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Meta has agreed to pay states up to  $17 billion in penalties as part of a settlement agreement &lt;/strong&gt;for a landmark federal child safety lawsuit. California, Colorado, Kentucky and New Jersey accused Meta of designing Facebook and Instagram to be addictive for children, claiming the company knew the risks but hid that information from the public. Meta denies these allegations. A portion of the &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/26/nx-s1-5944781/meta-settlement-child-safety-lawsuit" target="_blank"&gt;proposed settlement is expected to&lt;/a&gt; be allocated to support youth mental health programs, after-school activities and crisis intervention services. Meta also agreed to make changes to its platforms, including a default daily time limit of two hours for users under 18, a default block on nighttime use and a ban on displaying the number of likes and reactions on posts.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-140202" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 8608;         --source-height: 4842;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8608x4842+0+449/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7e%2F84%2F0799dcde42459af58848bd15cc39%2Fgettyimages-2290506003.jpg" class="img" alt="California Attorney General Rob Bonta speaks to reporters at a press conference after opening arguments took place in the Meta trial over a lawsuit brought by US states about social media addiction at the Ronald V. Dellums Federal Building and United States Courthouse in Oakland, California, on August 18, 2026." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 California Attorney General Rob Bonta speaks to reporters at a press conference after opening arguments took place in the Meta trial over a lawsuit brought by US states about social media addiction at the Ronald V. Dellums Federal Building and United States Courthouse in Oakland, California, on August 18, 2026.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          GODOFREDO A. VASQUEZ/AFP via Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;It could take years to implement these changes&lt;/strong&gt;, psychologist Mitch Prinstein tells NPR's Rhitu Chatterjee. Prinstein, who was set to testify at the trial on Monday, says that constant notifications about likes and dislikes don't suit the developing pre-teen and teen brain. At that age, children are increasingly focused on popularity and &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-5946263:x" target="_blank"&gt;hypersensitive to social signals&lt;/a&gt;, he says.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;The settlement is among the largest ever struck by a U.S. company.&lt;/strong&gt; Here's what it could &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/27/nx-s1-5945278/meta-settlement-child-safety-big-tech" target="_blank"&gt;mean for the rest of the tech industry&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A federal judge in Boston yesterday canceled her nationwide ruling that blocked &lt;/strong&gt;the U.S. Postal Service from following President Trump's executive order limiting mail-in voting, giving the administration another temporary win in its legal battle ahead of the midterm election. U.S. District Judge Indira Talwani's decision comes after the Supreme Court earlier this month ruled that a similar ruling by Talwani, which applied only to 23 mainly Democratic-led states and Washington, D.C., was made too early, before the USPS finalized plans based on Trump's directives. This latest development sets the stage for &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/26/nx-s1-5945260/trump-mail-in-voting-ballots" target="_blank"&gt;another potential Supreme Court review&lt;/a&gt; of the president's executive order. So far, the high court has addressed only procedural issues, without ruling on the legality of Trump's order.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Nuclear Regulatory Commission is preparing to eliminate a 50-year-old rule &lt;/strong&gt;designed to minimize radiation exposure. The update aligns with the Trump administration's expansive changes to nuclear oversight and efforts to promote nuclear energy to power data centers for artificial intelligence. The proposed rule would eliminate the standard known as "As Low As Reasonably Achievable" (ALARA), which requires the nuclear industry to reduce workers' radiation exposure to levels below the stated limits where possible. The new rule would also allow certain workers to receive higher radiation exposures under specific conditions. While some view the removal of ALARA as a long-overdue improvement, critics warn that it &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/27/nx-s1-5920368/nrc-nuclear-radiation-safety-rules-alara-update" target="_blank"&gt;could lead to increased radiation exposure&lt;/a&gt;. The public comment period ends on Aug. 31, and the rule could take effect early next year.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Massive flash floods in Nepal and Tibet have killed at least 270 people. &lt;/strong&gt;More than &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/27/nx-s1-5946309/at-least-1-500-missing-after-deadly-nepal-tibet-floods" target="_blank"&gt;1,500 people are confirmed missing&lt;/a&gt;, including at least 65 Americans, according to the Nepal Tourism Board. The floods began when a glacier collapsed into a valley, blocking a river and causing water levels to surge some 30 feet in just 30 minutes, burying people and communities in mud.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Search-and-rescue efforts are still in the early stages&lt;/strong&gt;, NPR's Diaa Hadid says. Rescue workers are trying to dig people out of the mud, but washed-out roads are hindering the progress, according to Riaz Lodhi, head of the World Food Program. Nepali authorities are well-practiced in emergency response, especially after experiences like the 2015 earthquake that killed around 9,000 people, aid workers say. But they say authorities still haven't seen anything of this magnitude. Experts warn that catastrophes like this are &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-5946263:x" target="_blank"&gt;likely to happen again&lt;/a&gt; because rising temperatures in the Himalayas, which are warming faster than the global average, destabilize the region's 60,000 glaciers and trigger more avalanches.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Deep dive&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-139749" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 6720;         --source-height: 3780;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/6720x3780+0+350/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fec%2F4f%2Ffae96d4c48709fd9fe30fb761c92%2Fgettyimages-2252050224.jpg" class="img" alt="Black man appearing middle aged sitting at desk talking to Black woman and Black child in office setting, principal discussing school matters with parent and student" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Black man appearing middle aged sitting at desk talking to Black woman and Black child in office setting, principal discussing school matters with parent and student                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          DragonImages/iStockphoto via Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;The U.S. Department of Education is investigating Fayetteville Public Schools in Arkansas for directing teachers to take students' racial identities into account when issuing discipline. The school system says this approach is part of an effort to bring about "restorative justice" to address "the disproportionality of discipline on students of color." The Department's Office for Civil Rights (OCR) has issued new guidance warning school leaders that they shouldn't consider a student's race in disciplinary matters, and failure to comply could lead to an investigation. This move revives a civil rights &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/25/nx-s1-5936474/discrimination-school-discipline-suspension-race-education-department-civil-rights" target="_blank"&gt;debate over discipline in K-12 schools&lt;/a&gt; and what counts as discrimination. Here's a dive into school discipline data over the years:&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🏫 In 2014, the Obama administration issued guidance arguing that U.S. school discipline data often revealed large, potentially discriminatory impacts on students of color. According to the data at that time, African American students made up 15% of the student population, yet they were 35% who were suspended at least once.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🏫 Following the 2014 guidance, many school districts shifted away from suspensions and expulsions, adopting restorative justice policies that prioritize dialogue over punishment.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🏫 By 2018, student suspensions had decreased from 5.6% to 4.7%, NPR reported. Hispanic students experienced a 30% reduction, while Black students and students with disabilities also saw significant drops.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;🏫 The Trump administration's latest guidance argues that previous policies led schools to cover up student misconduct to avoid federal scrutiny for any racial disparities.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Picture show&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-140110" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 8175;         --source-height: 4598;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2