com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/8175x4598+0+426/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F7f%2Fa7%2Fc3ea694445c5ac45c56c48fb48ed%2F260513-fypseries-carquinezmodelrailroad-21-bl.jpg" class="img" alt="Members repair tracks, operate model trains and chat at the Carquinez Model Railroad Society." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Members repair tracks, operate trains and chat at the Carquinez Model Railroad Society in Crockett, Calif., on May 13, 2026. The volunteer-run organization maintains an expansive model railroad layout based on Bay Area railroad towns and hosts public open houses throughout the year.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Beth LaBerge/KQED&lt;br&gt;                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Train enthusiasts have found their people in Crockett, Calif., through the Carquinez Model Railroad Society (CMRS), where they can design, build, and unleash their creativity. The club has nearly 180 members. They host popular open houses every few months, allowing the public to marvel at model trains that wind through in miniature versions of Oakland, Calif., and Sparks, Nev., on more than a mile of track. But the fun doesn't stop there. Members maintain the club's 129-year-old building, construct new stretches of track and fix each other's trains. Whether you're a die-hard train lover or someone who appreciates intricate miniatures, step into the world of CMRS with &lt;a href="https://www.kqed.org/arts/13992806/carquinez-model-railroad-society-trains-crockett-vallejo" target="_blank"&gt;captivating photos of the members' creations&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;3 things to know before you go&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-140279" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 2251;         --source-height: 1266;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2251x1266+925+235/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1c%2Fc5%2F26d0894140a3aef3434f609381f8%2Fgettyimages-630910611.jpg" class="img" alt="Tim Curry played Dr. Frank-N-Furter, the tyrannical gender-bending alien scientist in both stage and screen versions of The Rocky Horror Picture Show." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Tim Curry played Dr. Frank-N-Furter, the tyrannical gender-bending alien scientist in both stage and screen versions of &lt;em&gt;The Rocky Horror Picture Show.&lt;/em&gt;                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Movie Poster Image Art/Moviepix/Getty Images                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ol class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;Actor Tim Curry &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/26/nx-s1-5477607/tim-curry-dead" target="_blank"&gt;died on Tuesday&lt;/a&gt; at the age of 80. He was well known for his role as gender-bending scientist Dr. Frank-N-Furter in &lt;em&gt;The Rocky Horror Picture Show&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;In this week's installment of Far-Flung Postcards, NPR's Jane Arraf visits Karbala, Iraq, where more than 20 million Shia Muslims &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/26/g-s1-138982/karbala-iraq-arbaeen-shia-muslims" target="_blank"&gt;gathered to commemorate Arbaeen&lt;/a&gt;. It marks the killing of Imam Hussein, grandson of the Prophet Muhammad, 14 centuries ago.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Scientists rescued 34 endangered humped tree snails from Guam by &lt;a href="https://www.stlpr.org/health-science-environment/2026-08-26/how-a-typhoon-and-peanut-butter-jars-brought-rare-snails-to-st-louis" target="_blank"&gt;collecting them in empty peanut butter jars&lt;/a&gt; ahead of a powerful typhoon. The snails are now at the St. Louis Zoo. (via &lt;a href="https://www.stlpr.org/" target="_blank"&gt;STLPR&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This newsletter was edited by &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/people/859721331/suzanne-nuyen"target="_blank"   &gt;&lt;em&gt;Suzanne Nuyen&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
